{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790470765,"nonce":"JdmQNRakoRythEDxooe8B7eawqk=","title":"Python: unpacking a range held as a dynamic value gives the count when there are too many","message":"A range() value is a list of ints once it leaves the expression it was written in, so `v = range(3); a, b = v` with `v` dynamic (or any unpack of a range passed through a parameter) raises \"too many values to unpack (expected 2, got 3)\" where CPython 3.14 prints \"(expected 2)\". An unpack of `range(...)` written in the assignment itself gives CPython's message (e64fde52). Matching the dynamic case needs a range to stay distinguishable from a list at run time (a range kind of box, or a range type), which also bears on `type(range(3))` and `repr(range(3))`.","files":null}]}